Part 1: Step-by-Step Guide for 21.json through 100.json (Pune)
We have built a dedicated universal import tool: 

src/scripts/import/importCityDataset.js

Because the import is completely idempotent, running it over the entire folder data/Pune will:

Detect that listings from 1.json to 20.json (the first 200) already exist in MongoDB and leave them untouched.
Process only the new listings from 21.json through 100.json (the remaining 800 listings).
Provision the 649 new owner user accounts without touching existing accounts.
Auto-curate the 70 new Pune localities into the 

Area
 collection.
Step 1: Take a Pre-Import Baseline Snapshot (Safety First)
Before writing new records, capture a snapshot of the current database state:

bash
node src/scripts/backup/backupDatabaseState.js
What this does: Captures current document counts, indexes, and primary ID manifests in data/backups/latest_snapshot.json. If anything unexpected happens, you have an exact record of the baseline state.

Step 2: Run a Dry-Run Simulation (Zero Risk)
Always test first without modifying the database:

bash
node src/scripts/import/importCityDataset.js --city=Pune --dir=data/Pune --dry-run
What this does:

Scans all 100 JSON files (1.json to 100.json).
Validates JSON syntax, envelope structure, and schema constraints.
Maps room configurations, amenities, contact details, photos, and SEO slugs.
Outputs a complete transformation summary showing:
Total files scanned (100)
Total listings to import (1,000)
New owner profiles to create (649)
New Pune localities to create (70)
Validation failures (0)
Step 3: Execute the Live Import (--apply)
When the dry-run output is verified, execute the live database write:

bash
node src/scripts/import/importCityDataset.js --city=Pune --dir=data/Pune --apply
What this does:

Resolves City: Links Pune's cityId (6a8698dfcfb45c57ac09ad31).
Upserts Areas: Inserts the 70 new localities under Pune with standardized URL slugs.
Provisions Owners: Creates the 649 new owner user accounts with roles ['owner', 'pg_owner'] while honoring MongoDB sparse index rules.
Batched Property Writes: Uses bulkWrite with { ordered: false } in chunks of 100 properties directly into the unified properties collection.
Generates Report: Writes a detailed markdown report into data/reports/pune_import_report_<timestamp>.md.
Step 4: Verify the Database Integrity
Run the automated validation suite to verify the imported records:

bash
node src/scripts/import/validateImportedData.js
Checks Performed:

Properties count matches expected baseline + imported count.
0 null-island coordinates ([0, 0]).
0 duplicate slugs.
All properties have valid cityId, areaId, and owner references.
All room configurations and photo URLs are populated.
Step 5: (Optional) Rollback Procedure
If you ever need to cleanly reverse the import without touching pre-existing listings:

bash
# Dry run rollback:
node src/scripts/import/rollbackPuneImport.js --dry-run
# Live rollback:
node src/scripts/import/rollbackPuneImport.js --apply
Part 2: How to Store Data for ANY Other City (e.g. Mumbai, Bengaluru, Delhi)
The import pipeline is designed to work across all 1,794 Indian cities seeded in our database.

Step 1: Check City Name in the Database
Verify that the target city exists in the cities collection:

bash
node -e "
require('dotenv').config();
const mongoose = require('mongoose');
mongoose.connect(process.env.MONGODB_URI).then(async () => {
  const city = await mongoose.connection.db.collection('cities').findOne({ name: { \$regex: /^mumbai$/i } });
  console.log('City found:', city ? { id: city._id, name: city.name, state: city.state } : 'NOT FOUND');
  await mongoose.disconnect();
});
"
(Replace mumbai with any city like bangalore, hyderabad, chennai, delhi, kolkata, etc.)

NOTE

All 1,794 Indian cities from data/cities.json are already active in the database with their coordinates and state metadata.

Step 2: Create the City Data Folder & Place JSON Files
Create a subfolder under data/ matching the city name:

bash
mkdir -p data/Mumbai
Place your JSON files inside (1.json, 2.json, 3.json, etc.).
The pipeline accepts any of the following JSON formats:

Standard envelope: { "success": true, "data": [{ "data": [ ...PG items... ] }] }
Direct array: [ ...PG items... ]
Object with array: { "properties": [ ... ] } or { "data": [ ... ] }
Step 3: Run the Dry-Run for the New City
Run the simulation specifying --city and --dir:

bash
node src/scripts/import/importCityDataset.js --city=Mumbai --dir=data/Mumbai --dry-run
The script will automatically:

Find Mumbai in the cities collection and retrieve its canonical cityId and state.
Inspect all localities (e.g., Andheri West, Bandra, Powai, BKC) and identify which ones need to be added to the 

Area
 collection under Mumbai.
Inspect owner contacts and prepare User accounts.
Calculate pricing, room types, photos, and slugs (e.g., zolo-stay-andheri-west-mumbai-a1b2c3).
Step 4: Apply the Import Live
When satisfied with the dry-run summary, execute:

bash
node src/scripts/import/importCityDataset.js --city=Mumbai --dir=data/Mumbai --apply
The data is committed directly into the unified properties collection.

Step 5: Test Search Queries on the New City
Verify that search, filtering, and geospatial queries work immediately:

bash
node -e "
require('dotenv').config();
const mongoose = require('mongoose');
mongoose.connect(process.env.MONGODB_URI).then(async () => {
  const Property = require('./src/models/Property.model');
  const count = await Property.countDocuments({ city: { \$regex: /^mumbai$/i }, status: 'approved' });
  console.log('Approved properties in Mumbai:', count);
  const sample = await Property.findOne({ city: { \$regex: /^mumbai$/i } }).select('title area pricing.expectedPrice slug');
  console.log('Sample Listing:', sample);
  await mongoose.disconnect();
});
"
Part 3: Architecture & Quality Guarantees
Feature	How It Operates
Idempotency	Uses MongoDB bulkWrite with updateOne and $set based on _id. Running the script multiple times updates existing documents without creating duplicates.
Locality Management	Localities in JSON are automatically converted into 

Area
 documents linked to the parent cityId with indexed slugs.
Owner Provisioning	Automatically creates owner accounts with roles: ['owner', 'pg_owner']. Explicitly omits empty email/phone fields so MongoDB's sparse unique indexes are never violated.
SEO Slugs	Generates human-readable, collision-free slugs (e.g. property-name-locality-city-hexid) using 

slug.util.js
.
Geospatial Readiness	Stores coordinates as GeoJSON Point [longitude, latitude], allowing immediate $near radius and boundary queries via the 2dsphere index.


